## 1. Why it’s *possible*  
Your background in advanced English shows you already have strong analytical and communication skills—both crucial for physics. Many physics programs accept students who start with a non‑science under‑graduate; the key is to build a solid math & physics foundation *before* applying.

---

## 2. Road‑map Overview

| Phase | Goal | Key Activities | Time Frame (per year) |
|-------|------|----------------|-----------------------|
| **Year 0–1** | Build fundamentals | Calculus, Linear Algebra, Classical Mechanics, Intro to Quantum | 1–2 years |
| **Year 1–2** | Deepen physics | Electromagnetism, Statistical Mechanics, Modern Physics, Computational Methods | 1–2 years |
| **Year 2–3** | Specialized prep | Advanced Classical Mechanics, Quantum Field Theory (QFT), General Relativity (GR) | 1–2 years |
| **Year 3–4** | Graduate‑ready | Research project, Physics‑focused electives, GRE/Subject tests, Grad‑school applications | 1–2 years |
| **Post‑grad** | Master’s & beyond | Master’s coursework + thesis / research | 2 years |

> **Total:** ~4–6 years of self‑study, then 2‑year master’s program.

